  "slug": "quantum-topic-350f",
  "summary": "New preprints pair adaptive classical ansätze with quantum hardware data or early-FTQC algorithms to extend many-body dynamics and ground-state estimation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is algorithmic: making correlated quantum dynamics and ground-state problems tractable through adaptivity or hybrid quantum-classical input. On the classical side, multiset tree tensor networks with per-configuration bond-dimension adaptation yield converged exciton dynamics in Holstein-type models. An adaptive time-dependent ghost Gutzwiller scheme extends Hubbard quench dynamics to longer times and matches DMFT references across coupling strengths. Thermofield dynamics combined with ML-MCTDH and finite-basis Bogoliubov transforms handles anharmonic Morse baths for vibrational relaxation. For neural quantum states, an implicit-Euler stochastic reconfiguration underpins implicit augmented imaginary-time evolution. Hybrid approaches include Rydberg-array measurements used to pretrain RBM and RNN guiding wavefunctions for projective QMC, an early-FTQC algorithm estimating small energy differences such as molecular forces with lower sampling cost than phase estimation, and quantum-assisted ground-state estimation within simulated annealing to compress many-fermion bases.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
